@@ -98,6 +98,8 @@ decided together and fixed in one pass; see [§1.2](#12-the-five-parked-defects)

 ### 1.1 Fixed

+**D168 — DEVDESK-629 (F1, F3, F5, F7, F2): Nexus corrections from NX-04 and NX-05; point 1 (token realm policy) is implemented by DEVDESK-630. Decided (user, story DEVDESK-629, 2026-11-20; plan `.claude/plans/DEVDESK-629-plan.md`). (1) **A Bearer realm off the origin is never followed with credentials.** The token exchange (`exchangeBearerTokenContext`, the only such site in the product) refuses, before any request, a realm whose scheme, host or port differ from the request that received the challenge, **when credentials are configured** (username and password both set, the condition under which Basic is sent); without credentials the anonymous token request goes out as before. Typed error `oci.ErrTokenRealmOffOrigin` (`token realm on another host (<host>)`, test with `errors.Is`), naming the realm's host only, never its userinfo, path or query; not a `StatusError`. The origin is compared as `sameOrigin` does (exact scheme and `u.Host`): `host` and `host:443` differ, `http` and `https` differ (refuse when in doubt, as D156). Two frozen exceptions (`realmExceptions` in `internal/oci/realm.go`, pinned by `TestRealmExceptionsAreFrozen`), one way only (registry to realm), https on both sides, host compared exactly with no port: Docker Hub (`registry-1.docker.io` to `auth.docker.io`) and **GitLab.com (`registry.gitlab.com` to `gitlab.com`, whose registry names `https://gitlab.com/jwt/auth` as its realm)**. A self-hosted GitLab registry whose realm is on the GitLab host stays refused: a per-configuration exception is a follow-up story. Rejected: following the realm with the credentials removed (an anonymous token is useless to an authenticated caller and would hide the refusal); an exception per configuration (follow-up). Reason: the `X-Forwarded-Host` probe of NX-05 shows that a Nexus behind a reverse proxy announces the host it is told, so a realm is not trustworthy by itself. Display: footer, level Error, `token realm on another host` (tags opened from the search, tags opened from the form, which used to be silent, and image search); verdict `failed` in the search. Other callers (`imageupdate`, signature, remediation) have no footer: journal and column unchanged. Amends D156 (the guards stopped at the scheme of the realm) and the pin of `TestMeasuredRealmStaysOnTheRegistryOrigin` (the frozen note `docs/measurements/2026-11-20-nexus-access.md` describes the code before this change and is not edited). (2) The 404 `Remote Auto Blocked` of a proxy whose upstream is cut is `failed`, temporary, detected on the JSON `message` with a body read up to 4 KiB (step 3). (3) The timeout message reads `no answer within 15 s` from `oci.RequestTimeout`, delay unchanged (step 4). (4) Docker credentials of an entry with a path: exact key, then `host[:port]` as written (step 6). (5) `Cached only` becomes `May omit images`, carried by the type of the discovered member (steps 7 and 8). Limits: see the plan (R1 to R11). Visible effect of (1): a registry whose realm is on another host and that is configured with credentials now fails with `token realm on another host` instead of authenticating; the same registry without credentials is unchanged. No ADR: reversible by a revert, the cache field of (5) is additive. **Plane:** DEVDESK-629, DEVDESK-630 (task 629.1). Number: D167 is the highest on `main` on 2026-11-20; re-check before merge. Sources: `internal/oci/realm.go`, `internal/oci/registry.go`, `internal/ui/oci_resources/registry_failure.go`, `internal/ui/oci_resources/browser_bridge.go`, `internal/ui/oci_resources/browser_images.go`, `docs/architecture/network.md`; D156.**
+
 **D167 — DEVDESK-579 (NX-12): a Nexus group member's catalog is read under its path. Decided (user, 2026-11-20, from the plan `.claude/plans/DEVDESK-579-plan.md`). Context: the NX-03 measurement (`docs/measurements/2026-11-17-nexus-catalog.md`, Nexus 3.98.1-01) contradicts H1 (prefixed names) and IS-07 (several members of one host sharing one `/v2/_catalog`). There is no catalogue at the root of the HTTP port. The catalogue of a hosted or proxy repository is served at `/v2/<repository>/_catalog` (200, names not prefixed). The same path answers 404 on the group (correlated with `pathEnabled`, not tested). A group's dedicated connector returns the union of its members, with no attribution. Decided: (1) Approach (a): `/v2/<prefix>/_catalog`, each name rebuilt as `<prefix>/<name>`. Rejected: (b) `/repository/<name>/v2/_catalog` (the form D39 removed: it finds images that cannot be pulled); (c) the Nexus search API (NX-10, kept for a restricted account, DEVDESK-513; effort L; it sends the typed text to the server, against IS-03); (d) changing nothing. (2) Only the entries whose group is declared `provider: nexus` are concerned (discovered members with a prefix, and `parent:` entries). No new configuration field, no automatic fallback on a 404 (declared, never sniffed). (3) One walk per member: `walkKey` includes the catalogue prefix. This amends D140 points 4 and 9. (4) Option 3-A: discovered members with no address of their own, on a group's connector, become ONE search unit, labelled `<alias> (group)`, label validated by the UX (alternatives `nx/*`, `(all)` and `(members)` rejected). The suffix is never truncated (`datatable.Column.KeepSuffix`), and a help phrase is shown on `?`. Selecting in the picker is bypassed by design. An undiscovered group is unchanged. (5) Security: the prefix is validated before any request (empty segments, `.`, `..`, backslash, control characters, blanks; length bounds 255 and 1024). The `Link` next page is followed only on the member's own path. (6) Fixtures: the 12 measured answers (`internal/nexusfixture/fixtures/catalog/`) and the closed path list `recordPaths` extended to the path form. Limits and risks: the `?n=1000` tolerance of fakenexus on a path is an inference, not measured. A 404 of the group with `?n=1000` is not tolerated, so the criterion 6 test on the group is Skip (DEVDESK-580). `validRepoPath` refuses upper case: catalogue names with upper case are ignored and an upper-case prefix is refused; not measured if Nexus serves a repository with upper-case letters. A restricted account is not exercised (risk of a false « no result »). The catalogue of a proxy lists the upstream, not its cache: this contradicts the `Cached only` label (NX-09). Other versions, reverse proxies and HTTPS are not measured. Visible effect: ticking a single bare member now searches the whole group. No ADR: reversible by a revert, with no schema or disk cache change. **Plane:** DEVDESK-579 (NX-12); follow-ups DEVDESK-580 (NX-08b) and DEVDESK-513 (NX-10). Number: D166 is the highest on `main` and in the worktree; no other open PR takes D167 (checked on 2026-11-20), but a parallel session could. Sources: `internal/oci/catalog.go`, `internal/imagesearch/catalog.go`, `internal/imagesearch/walks.go`, `internal/registrymgr/nexus.go`, `internal/ui/oci_resources/registry_browser.go`, `internal/nexusfixture/fixtures/catalog/`, `internal/nexusbench/record.go`, `docs/measurements/2026-11-17-nexus-catalog.md`; D39, D135 (IS-03, `repo_prefix` and R1), D140, D162 point 7(b), D166.**

 **D166 — DEVDESK-578 (NX-07b): a Nexus 404 `Repository not found` on the detailed group endpoint is a members refusal. Decided (user, 2026-11-19, from the DEVDESK-578 brief). Context: the NX-03 measurement (`docs/measurements/2026-11-17-nexus-catalog.md`) showed that the pull account gets a 404 with the body `{"id":"*","message":"\"Repository not found\""}` on the detailed group endpoint, not the 403 D161 assumed. NX-07 (DEVDESK-510) therefore did not reach its result: the refusal showed as `unreachable`. (1) That 404 is requalified as `*MembersRefusedError{Status: 404, Cause: *oci.StatusError}` in `internal/registrymgr`, and only at the detailed step of the discovery, after a generic 200 `type: group` reply. (2) Only a 404 whose JSON body has `message` equal to « Repository not found » qualifies: inner quotes and surrounding spaces removed, case ignored. Any other 404 stays `unreachable`, so an absent route (an older Nexus, another format, an HTML 404 from a proxy) is not reported as a refusal. The body is read up to 4 KiB and is never put in an error or a log. Assumption: the measured body is about 50 bytes; the shape of a 404 for an absent route is not measured. (3) The 404 refusal is persisted in `refusals` with `status: 404`, as the 403 is. Criterion 2 of the story (« nothing in the members cache ») is read as: the member list is never written by a refusal. This is the user's decision; criterion 4 (« identical to the 403 ») takes precedence. (4) The UI labels of DEVDESK-510 are unchanged: `denied` in the Members cell, `unreachable` for a transient failure. Limits and risks: a group that really does not exist returns the same body, so a group deleted between the two calls reads `denied` until the next successful `ctrl+r`; if Nexus changes the message, the 404 falls back to `unreachable` with no data lost, and a change of the message only shows when the fixtures are regenerated (`mise run nexus-bench:record`), when `TestDetectGroupOnMeasuredNexusAnswers` and `TestMeasuredRESTAnswersHoldThePremises` fail (the staleness test only compares version and edition with `internal/nexusbench/descriptor.json`); without regeneration the behavior falls back to `unreachable`, with no damage; on a Nexus where an administrator would receive this 404, the state reads `denied`, but the `repo_prefix` advice stays correct. Sources: `internal/registrymgr/nexus.go` (`repositoryNotFoundError`, `isRepositoryNotFound`, `refusedStatus`, `nexusGET`), `internal/registrymgr/refusal.go`, `docs/measurements/2026-11-17-nexus-catalog.md`; D161, D156, D23.**
